  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of the tripartite constituents to design and implement policies to promote gender equality and tackle discrimination, and violence and harassment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Violence and harassment in the world of work remain underreported in North Macedonia, with limited data available on its prevalence. A 2019 study found that gender-based discrimination disproportionately affects women, particularly in the private sector. Many workers, especially those with lower education levels, are unaware that such discrimination is illegal. Sexual harassment is widespread, yet only a small percentage of victims report incidents due to normalization, lack of trust in enforcement mechanisms, and fear of stigmatization. Traditional gender roles and stereotypes further contribute to its acceptance.
An analysis in 2021 revealed inconsistencies between national legislation and ILO Convention No. 190 (C.190), despite an otherwise comprehensive legal framework. The ratification of C.190 in October 2023 marked a key milestone, requiring alignment of national laws with the Convention&#x2019;s provisions. The ILO has supported efforts to strengthen protections, including the development of risk assessment tools for violence, harassment, and psychosocial risks.
Key priorities for ILO assistance include technical support to draft a new law on mobbing and harassment at work. A tripartite working group established in 2022 has been revitalized to ensure that the new legislation integrates C.190 provisions. 
To promote implementation, the ILO supports a pilot program for applying risk assessment tools in enterprises. In partnership with the Organisation of Employers of Macedonia, an awareness campaign and training workshops is conducted for businesses, workers&#x2019; organizations, OSH specialists, and Ministry of Labour staff. Companies committed to applying the tools receive support throughout the process.
Additionally, the ILO supports the development of training materials based on the ILO ACTEMP Guide violence and harassment to train HR staff and managers on workplace violence and harassment. The materials are tested in multiple countries, including North Macedonia.</narrative>
  </description>
